course: everyday
lesson: 358
title: Promises to family
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Oliver in Sydney promised his little brother Alfie they would build a Lego rocket after school. Then his mates knocked and he went to the skate park. Alfie waited by the window all afternoon with the box open. When Oliver came home, Alfie was crying and Mum just looked sad. Oliver said sorry, and on Saturday he played with Alfie the whole morning — the rocket, two spaceships and a moon base.
explain: Maya promised to feed the cat while Mum was at work and forgot. The cat meowed all night and Mum was cross. Maya grumbled «it's only one dinner» and went to bed in a huff, but in the morning she apologised, fed him first thing, and set a reminder on the kitchen clock so it would not happen again.
explain: A promise is your word that someone can plan around. To a little brother, a gran or a cat, a broken promise means waiting, disappointment or hunger.
explain: Lucy promised to tidy her room before going out and didn't, so she could not go out the next day. Sam forgot to call Gran, who waited by the phone all evening; he called her the next day and said sorry. Will went to the park instead of helping Dad wash the car; he said sorry and helped the next week.
explain: If you cannot keep a promise, say so before, not after: a call or a «sorry, can we do Saturday instead?» is honest. A no-show leaves the other person guessing.
fact: A promise is a commitment that another person relies on.
fact: Promises to small children matter as much as promises to adults; they remember and wait.
fact: Pets depend completely on their owners for food and water.
fact: If you need to break a plan, telling the person in advance is kinder than not turning up.
fact: Telling a secret you promised to keep can hurt a friendship badly.
fact: Something you borrow is a promise to give it back.
fact: Changing a habit, such as teasing, usually takes many tries.
fact: Reminders, like a note or an alarm, help people keep promises when they are forgetful.
fact: Keeping small promises builds trust, so people believe your bigger ones.
exercise: Why did Oliver break his promise to Alfie? | He wanted to go out with his friends | Alfie told him he didn't want to play anyway | The Lego was lost | Mum asked him to go out
exercise: What happened while Oliver was out? | Alfie cried at the window | Alfie built the rocket alone happily | Alfie forgot all about the promise | Alfie went out with his own friends
exercise: What put things right with Alfie? | Saying sorry and playing with him all Saturday morning | Buying him a new Lego set | Telling him that promises to little ones don't really count | Inviting him to the skate park next time
exercise: Maya forgot to feed the cat. Why does that matter so much? | The cat depends on her for food | Cats can open the cupboard themselves | Cats only eat once a week | It doesn't matter, cats hunt mice
exercise: What did Maya do so it would not happen again? | She set a reminder | She asked for a different pet | She left the cat outside | She blamed the cat for not meowing
exercise: Isaac promised to keep his friend's secret and told someone to sound interesting. What happened? | The whole class knew, and his friend was hurt | His friend thanked him for sharing it with everyone | Nobody believed the secret | The secret stayed safe
exercise: Lucy promised to tidy her room before going out and did not. What was the consequence? | She could not go out the next day | Mum tidied it and gave her a treat | She got extra pocket money | Nothing happened
exercise: Sam promised Gran he'd call and forgot. Gran waited by the phone. What was the best fix? | Call her the next day and say sorry | Wait until her birthday to call | Send a text a week later saying he had been busy | Ask Mum to call instead
exercise: Will promised to help Dad wash the car and went to the park instead. What put it right? | Saying sorry and helping the next week | Saying the car was clean enough | Washing his bike instead | Paying Dad a pound
exercise: Ella promised to go to her friend's party but went to a different party. What would have been honest? | Telling him beforehand that she couldn't come | Not saying anything at all and just not turning up | Saying she was ill when she wasn't | Going to both parties for one minute
exercise: Noah borrowed his friend's game and kept it, even though his friend asked for it back. Why is that wrong? | Borrowing is a promise to give it back | Games belong to whoever has them | His friend had far too many games and would not miss one | It is fine after a month
exercise: Jake promised to stop teasing his sister and did it again. What is true about habits? | They take many tries to change | They change after just one promise | They never really change at all | They only change if you get a reward
exercise: Why does keeping small promises matter? | It builds trust so people believe you | Small promises don't matter | It makes you look clever | It means you never have to promise again
exercise: Oliver's friend says «he's only six, he'll forget». What is true? | Small children remember and wait | Six-year-olds forget promises at once | Promises to little ones don't count | Only promises in writing really count
exercise: Maya said «it's only one dinner». What is wiser? | One missed dinner still left the cat hungry | One dinner doesn't matter at all to a cat | Cats prefer to skip meals | It's fine if the cat is fat
test: «said I'd play with my little bro but the lads are at the park, he won't care». What's true? | He will care; tell him and come back, or keep it | He's too little to care | Little kids never wait for you, they find something else to do | Just go, he'll forget
test: Mum texts at 9pm: «Did you feed Biscuit??» You forgot. Best reply? | «No, sorry! Doing it now» | «Yes» and then feed him later when you remember | «He's fine, he's fat» | Don't reply
test: Gran: «I sat by the phone all evening, love». You forgot to call. Best move? | Say sorry and call her properly now | Say the phone was broken | Say you were doing homework | Say she should have called you
test: «I told everyone Sara's secret but she made me promise, so technically…». What's true? | Breaking the promise hurt her; say sorry | Technically it's fine | Secrets are made to share | Promises only really count if you write them down
test: mate: «you said you'd come to my party, where were you?!». You went to another one. Best fix? | Say sorry, and next time tell him beforehand | Say you were ill in bed and couldn't come | Say his party was boring | Say you forgot
test: broke my promise to wash dad's car last week and nothing happened, «it worked last time, so it's fine». Is it? | No, Dad was let down and trust gets thinner | Yes, if nothing happened last time then it's fine | Yes, cars wash themselves in rain | Only if Dad doesn't ask
test: friend: «gimme my game back, you said a week, it's been a MONTH». Best move? | Give it back today and say sorry | Say you lost it somewhere and you'll look for it | Say he gave it to you | Ask for another week
test: Mum: «You promised you'd stop teasing her and she's crying again». What's the honest reply? | «Sorry, I did it again. I'll keep trying» | «She started it, she always starts it» | «It was only a joke» | «I never promised»
test: You can't make it to Gran's on Sunday after all. When should you tell her? | As soon as you know | Late on Sunday afternoon | Never, she'll guess anyway | After she's cooked the dinner
test: Why might Alfie stop believing Oliver's promises? | Broken promises make people stop trusting your word | Little children can't understand what a promise is at all | Promises are too long to remember | Alfie is too young to trust anyone
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a promise made to a young child ought to be honoured. | The child relies upon it and experiences disappointment if it is broken | Promises made to young children are legally binding upon the person who makes them | Young children do not remember promises | Promises to adults alone are significant
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the borrowing of an article? | It entails an undertaking to return the article | It transfers ownership after one week | It entitles the borrower to retain the article indefinitely | It imposes no obligation upon the borrower
exam: It is given that a person can no longer attend an event to which attendance was promised. Select the response consistent with courtesy. | The host is informed in advance of the event | The person fails to attend without any notice at all | The person attends for a single moment only and leaves | The person informs the host a week afterwards
exam: State the principal consequence of the habitual keeping of small promises. | Others come to trust the person's word | Promises cease to be necessary | The person comes to be excused from keeping larger promises | No consequence follows
